WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.530
With a runtime of 130 minutes, Thor: Ragnarok
isn't exactly a short movie, but it would

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.880
have been a lot longer if director Taika Waititi
had gotten his way.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.320
Much of Waititi's method relied on improv,
so he encouraged leading man Chris Hemsworth

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.160
to ad-lib with his co-stars, which meant that
tons of the material they filmed didn't end

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.620
up being used in the final film.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.359
While a good chunk of that footage probably
belonged on the cutting room floor, there

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.080
were some moments of pure gold that had to
be removed due to time restraints.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.340
Thanks to the home video extras, here's a
look at the scenes you didn't get to see in

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.020
Thor: Ragnarok.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.740
Playing charades

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:38.050
The Grandmaster, played hilariously by Jeff
Goldblum, teaches Topaz the meaning of hand

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.470
signals in one extended scene which carries
on after Loki and Valkyrie are tasked with

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:43.720
capturing the escaped Thor.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.980
When they fail to understand the meaning of
his "go" signal, he starts ranting about all

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.140
the ways to tell people to do something without
ever having to open your mouth.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.760
"This means 'go' did you get that?

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.940
That's the universal sign, was I not clear?

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.420
For heaven's sakes, what did you make of that?"

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.911
He then proceeds to teach Topaz about the
signals that he promises are universal, although

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.600
she struggles to grasp them.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:06.980
"What's this?"

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.640
"Dial it down."

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.740
"THAT'S dial it down."

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.740
Meeting the Grandmaster

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.280
The Grandmaster makes a bit more of an impression
on Thor in an extended scene showing their

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.040
first meeting.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.530
The scene begins with Thor, freshly arrived
in the Grandmaster's palace, trying to escape,

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.860
while the celestial being sends his many followers
after a frustrating flying creature.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.600
"There it goes!

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:34.820
Go quickly.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.220
Holy cats."

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.900
After everyone else departs, the Grandmaster
tells Thor about the obedience discs, the

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.660
chip implanted in his neck that keeps him
from escaping.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.960
"It's a little invention of mine, by the way.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.700
You know, some people find it kind of erotic."

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.460
You know this part: the Grandmaster turns
his attention to Thor, asking the hero to

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.460
introduce himself.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.560
With a great amount of effort, Thor identifies
himself as the god of thunder before The Grandmaster

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.900
then takes Thor to see his interrogation of
his cousin Carlo.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:03.920
His crimes?

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.260
"A small camel."

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.580
"He said it was only a small camel."

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.700
"What did you do to a small camel?"

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:14.660
After a bit more back-and-forth, the clip
ends with Carlo being pardoned… from life.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.340
Thor gets used to his new roommate

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.620
Thor and the Hulk made a pretty great pair
in Thor: Ragnarok, but they didn't exactly

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:22.860
start out on good footing.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.620
After their big fight, the pair had to get
used to rooming together, and an extended

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.580
scene of the two in their quarters shows that
figuring out how to get along wasn't easy.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.180
The extended scene begins with Thor once again
trying to remove his obedience disc.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.099
The noise wakes up a sleeping Hulk, who has
to shake off a "bad dream" that seems to have

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.620
involved Bruce Banner trying to regain control
of his own body.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.419
Like the final film, Hulk ultimately wins
out and starts pestering his roommate for

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.500
seeming "sad."

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.440
Hulk antagonizes the title hero and eventually
knocks him over before Thor caves to admit

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.040
what's bothering him and starts kicking things
around the room.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.300
But if there's one thing you don't mess with,
it's Hulk's necklace.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.519
"Don't kick my beads!"

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.280
"I'll kick whatever I want to kick.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.100
Stomp on your stupid beads!"

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.680
And then, Hulk lets loose with the worst insult
he can think of.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.180
"Stupid idiot."

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.720
"You stupid white man!"

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:14.800
Ouch.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.120
Skurge finds Heimdall

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.740
Skurge went through a long journey in Thor:
Ragnarok, going from an undervalued soldier

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.580
to Hela's executioner to the savior of Asgard.

00:03:24.749 --> 00:03:28.000
Although the character was featured quite
a lot in the film, there was one scene that

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.840
didn't make the cut, showing him finding one
of Hela's most wanted — Heimdall.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.859
In the clip, Skurge appears to be trying to
get his bearings after Hela's destruction

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.680
of the Asgardian army when he spots a group
of people fleeing in the distance.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:44.340
He pulls out a pair of binoculars and trains
them on Heimdall, who turns around and looks

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.480
directly towards Skurge before fleeing.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.880
There's no word on why the filmmakers decided
not to keep the scene, as it would've hinted

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.380
early on that Skurge would be willing to defy
Hela's orders if it meant saving his friends.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.180
Still, the movie worked just fine without
the moment, and it made Skurge's eventual

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.960
change of heart even more surprising.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.480
Hulk chases Thor through Sakaar

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.360
Thor's escape from the Grandmaster's headquarters
was nothing short of heroic in the film, and

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.589
a deleted scene gives even more of a glimpse
at just what he had to go through to get out.

00:04:12.589 --> 00:04:16.239
In the clip, Thor jumps out the window of
the Grandmaster's palace, but he's followed

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.360
by an angry Hulk, who wants him to stay.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:22.120
Hulk crashes through the marketplace, greeted
with cheers from the locals everywhere he

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:22.620
goes.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.240
He follows a somersaulting, running Thor throughout
the colorful city until Thor finally makes

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.000
it to the junkyard where finds the former
Avengers ship.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.180
There isn't much to the scene, but it shows
more of Sakaar, and how its people react to

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.880
their champion, which starkly contrasts with
how he's regarded back on Earth.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.160
Thanks for watching!

